Materialize the supplied {{ skill_count }} SkillCard(s) on the current
GPU-kernel code surface. The current_kernel.problem and current_kernel.abi
objects in INPUT_JSON are the authoritative functional and calling contracts.
Preserve every declared
input/output property and semantic requirement. The carrier field is a
verbatim code-bearing hint, not an executable tool. Respect preconditions,
ordering, risks, target language, and target platform.
Bind requires/provides/conflicts to the same locus in the actual target code;
prior_actions alone does not establish a prerequisite. Preserve existing
mechanisms and implement the declared provides, including necessary layout and
thread mapping. Adapt carrier parameters to the target contract.
Use locus and forward_edit as the concrete source transition to adapt.

Produce a complete source bundle inside submission_dir. Every path must be a
normalized relative path: no absolute paths, "..", symbolic links, binary files,
or NUL bytes. Keep the bundle below 16 MiB.

{% if language|default('cuda') == 'cuda' %}
Use the AKO4X CUDA artifact layout, with exactly config.toml at the bundle root
and all implementation files under solution/. The generated config.toml defines
the entry point; the current kernel's legacy abi.interface is not the output
format. Its ABI inputs/outputs and problem remain authoritative.

Direct CUDA symbol export (recommended):
```toml
[solution]
name = "<candidate-name>"
definition = "<operator-name>"
author = "klineage"

[build]
language = "cuda"
entry_point = "kernel.cu::kernel"
destination_passing_style = true
```
Write solution/kernel.cu. Include <tvm/ffi/container/tensor.h>, receive
tvm::ffi::TensorView arguments, and export the host wrapper with
TVM_FFI_DLL_EXPORT_TYPED_FUNC(kernel, kernel). The evaluator appends preallocated
outputs to the ABI inputs, both in declaration order. Write outputs in place.

Python binding alternative: use entry_point = "binding.py::kernel" and
destination_passing_style = false in [build]. Write solution/binding.py with
def kernel(...), accepting ABI inputs directly and returning one output directly,
multiple outputs as a tuple, or None for no outputs. Do not define a loader.
For language = "cuda", the evaluator builds/loads all .cu/.c/.cc/.cpp/.cxx files
under solution/ before importing the binding. A binding can call raw CUDA through
torch custom ops registered by that compiled code. Use distinct source-derived
custom-op namespaces so candidate and reference can coexist.
For language = "python", the binding builds/loads its CUDA extension during
module import. Use installed tvm_ffi APIs if needed, not the obsolete
tvm.ffi Python import. All compilation/loading stays outside kernel(...).

Implement tensor computation in raw CUDA C/C++; Python is allocation, metadata,
and launch glue. Do not include CUTLASS/CuTe headers or delegate to expert
libraries. Build products stay outside the source bundle. Resolve source paths
relative to __file__; derive extension names from sources and build flags.

Validate tensor device, dtype, shape, and stride against the ABI. Guard the
tensor device and allocate outputs there. Submit every kernel and asynchronous
copy to the caller's current CUDA stream for that device; pass it explicitly
as the fourth chevron-launch argument. Do not substitute a default/new stream,
read tensor values on the CPU, or synchronize the device in the callable.
Propagate CUDA launch/runtime errors through the binding; never swallow them.
Recompute outputs from each call's inputs; do not cache or replay output values.
Do not add input generators, benchmark configuration/code, ITERATIONS.md,
per-iteration commits, or framework arithmetic fallbacks.
{% else %}
Use the declared python-callable-v1 abi.interface: its module defines the named
zero-argument loader, which returns a callable accepting ABI inputs in order.
Return one output directly, multiple outputs as a tuple, or None for no outputs.
Build/load dependencies before the loader returns, outside timed invocation.
{% endif %}

Then return exactly {"done": true}. Do not report compile, correctness, or
performance claims; an external evaluator owns those gates.
